# Evidence and reasoning invariants

Preserve these distinctions in all documentation:

- logical pipeline ≠ repository ≠ Databricks job;
- run success ≠ freshness ≠ data quality;
- expectation ≠ baseline;
- normative requirement ≠ historical regularity;
- observation ≠ assessment;
- missing evidence ≠ observed absence;
- typical ≠ healthy;
- atypical ≠ degraded/defective;
- assessment ≠ causal explanation;
- correlation ≠ attribution ≠ confirmed cause;
- data lineage ≠ operational dependency ≠ deployment lineage;
- current topology ≠ historical topology.

Rules:

- Time/history is first-class; avoid current-state-only designs.
- Material conclusions must be traceable to evidence and provenance.
- Expectations are normative; Baselines are descriptive and may not be silently promoted into requirements.
- A Baseline-only deviation may be atypical but must not be labeled unhealthy/degraded without normative or explicit directional semantics.
- Missing telemetry must not be converted into zero/no-event; observed absence requires sufficient evidence coverage.
- Assessments must identify the Observation evidence and Expectation/Baseline versions forming their basis.
- Health dimensions remain separate by default; any overall roll-up needs explicit component Assessments and aggregation rules.
- Reassessment after late/corrected evidence preserves prior conclusions and provenance.
- Unknown, conflicting, non-comparable, unavailable, unauthorized, and insufficient-evidence states are valid outcomes.
- Never invent a root cause to make a report complete.
- Root-cause reasoning should consider upstream origin and downstream impact.
- A successful Databricks run may still produce stale, incomplete, anomalous, or normatively degraded data.
- Business-facing explanations and engineering detail must derive from the same evidence.
